window.docContents[45].resolve({"/Foundations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Cauchy-problems":{"contents":"Formalized: for smooth quasilinear scalar equations of any order,\nnoncharacteristic Cauchy data determine every derivative of a solution on the\ninitial hypersurface. For first-order analytic systems in normal form, the\nformal power-series solution exists and is unique over any commutative ring\ncontaining , majorized systems have majorized solutions, an\nexplicit scalar majorant converges, and the resulting analytic solution with\nzero initial data exists near the origin and is locally unique. The\nanalytic evolution theorem\nand its hypersurface form\nrestore general analytic data.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Foundational Coverage and Open Problems","header":"3.5.1.6. Cauchy problems","id":"/Foundations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Cauchy-problems"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Types-of-Conclusion":{"contents":"Four kinds of conclusion serve different purposes:\n\n* a residual identity computes the error of an ansatz;* a profile equivalence says the residual vanishes exactly when the reduced\nequations hold;* a solution theorem proves the original PDE on the pre-singular domain;* a blow-up or lifespan theorem proves norm divergence or a finite endpoint.\n\nA singularity result must connect an actual solution to norm divergence or a\nproved obstruction to continuation. For an ansatz, a sufficient profile\ncondition and its analytic hypotheses can establish the solution; a converse\nprofile equivalence is useful but not required. The explicit nonlinear-heat\ntheorem is both a solution theorem and a blow-up theorem; the virial and\nRiccati theorems are lifespan theorems for intervals carrying their stated\nhypotheses.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity","header":"6.7.1.4. Types of Conclusion","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Types-of-Conclusion"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Extension___-present-level-of-generality":{"contents":"The extension results answer three increasingly quantitative questions.\n\n1. A representative extension\nextends a measurable function and its weak derivatives, with almost-everywhere\nagreement on the original domain.2. A bounded extension operator\nmakes this choice linear and controls its norm on the completed spaces.3. A supported extension operator\nalso makes the extended function vanish almost everywhere outside a chosen\nambient set, as needed for compactness arguments.\n\nBridges convert an operator-level extension into the representative data\nneeded by mollification. The  construction supplies a first-order\nextension for every , together with a variant of prescribed\nsupport.\n\nFor every positive integer  and , a bounded open domain\nwith local  graph boundary also has a bounded linear\n extension. The proof flattens the boundary, applies weighted\nreflection, and assembles the pieces with a partition of unity. Its action\non the base function agrees with a bounded operator on all of ;\nrestriction recovers every weak derivative. This compatibility lets an\nevolution argument use the same extension for a trajectory and a less regular\ntime derivative. At , preservation of null sets controls chart\npullback in the essential-supremum norm. The weak chain rule is obtained\nlocally at finite exponent, so this construction does not require the false\nclaim that smooth functions are norm-dense in .\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Sobolev Coverage and Open Problems","header":"4.6.1.4. Extension: present level of generality","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Sobolev-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Extension___-present-level-of-generality"}});